#---
# purpose: an extern's declared return is its own FunctionContract applied to each activation's row, not a shared un-instantiated declaration
#---
#
# `dbg/1` forwards to `fz_dbg_value(t) :: t when t: any`, a prelude extern
# with no fz body of its own. An ordinary function's return picks up its
# argument's type because the BODY carries it there; an extern has no body,
# so the same relation comes from applying the extern's own contract to each
# activation's row -- the one call-instantiation calculator every specced
# function already uses (`FunctionContract::apply`, `Types::match_arrow`).
#
# `ascending/0` and `descending/0` are the same two calls in each order. Each
# call keys its own activation of `dbg/1` -- `inputs=[int]` and
# `inputs=[float]` -- and each activation defines its own return from its own
# row instead of both sharing whichever type happened to be interned first,
# which is what made the un-applied declaration order-dependent.
#
# Paper answer.
#
#   ascending/0   dbg(11); dbg(10.5)   => float
#   descending/0  dbg(10.5); dbg(11)   => int
#
# A function's return is the type of its last expression, so each of these is
# the return of its own final `dbg` call: `dbg(10.5)` for `ascending/0` and
# `dbg(11)` for `descending/0`. The two differ only because each call site's
# activation of the extern applies its contract to its own argument. Without
# that application both would read the same declared type and the answer
# would depend on which was interned first.
#
# Execution prints the boxed values dbg always prints, in call order.
def ascending() do
  dbg(11)
  dbg(10.5)
end

def descending() do
  dbg(10.5)
  dbg(11)
end

def main() do
  ascending()
  descending()
end
